Perhaps the most difficult part of making a new area is the file creation
process.  You need a mob file, an obj file, and a zone file.  Each of these
is detailed in their respective help entries.  (mob_file, obj_file, and
zone_file)

The zone file is the hardest of the external files to write, mainly because
it is totally different than the others, and as of this writing, there is no
"zone editor" for you to use.  Please read this through thoroughly, and read
it often, as the information contained will most likely solve your problems
before you have to ask an individual for help.  If you follow it through every
step, you will save yourself time and effort from re-writes and such when your
file is deemed unacceptable.

This moves from top to bottom of your file, detailing each section and the
different parts contained in each, and what you HAVE to put in, and what you
MAY put in if you need it, and most importantly, how NOT to do it.

// The following appear ONCE AND ONLY ONCE in each zone file!!

SAVE: <zone>    // Each zone file, mob file, obj file, and shop file
        // MUST start with this line, and MUST have the same field
        // for <zone> (in other words, name them ALL Norport, not
        // Norport Mobs, Norport Objs, etc...)

START ZONE  // If you don't have this, the auto-magic submission e-mail
        // parser will complain

// The following must appear at the start of EACH ZONE in your file!
// (will be explained later.)

ZONE "name" // The name of THIS zone (not this file.)  You may have
        // multiple zones in one file but they must be SUBZONES and
        // not totally seperate areas. (parts of a forest, for
        // instance)

<definitions>   // Valid definitions are as follows, they must appear after
        // the ZONE "name" but before any commands.  They can be in
        // any order.
  range (lo - hi)   // Every zone MUST HAVE a range marked out in the
            // form range (XXX000-XXX145) YES you need the XXX's
            // and YES your zones MUST begin on a 00 number (100,
            // 200, 300, etc...) Do not include empty rooms, when
            // you build the area fill up all the "holes"

  save (lo - hi)    // The room range here should match the one defined
            // in the 'range' definintion.  Do not omit this
            // field.

  reset never
  reset (always, <time>)
  reset (empty, <time>) // This is also required.  The words never, always, and
            // empty should be included AS PRESENT HERE, they are
            // keywords, not variables.  Never will never repop,
            // always will always repop after <time> minutes, and
            // empty will repop after <time> mins if there are no
            // players in the zone.
  boot_only { commands }    // This will perform the commands listed only
                // after a full reboot.  That means if you
                // load all your stuff in this field, once
                // it's gone, it's gone for good (practically.)

  zone_limit { limits } // If you have any random loads you will need to limit
            // the number of mobs/objs that are present in the
            // zone at any one time.  Limits are in the following
            // format:
    zone_limit {
      10 mob XXX001
      5 mob XXX002
      10 obj XXX001
      25 obj XXX002
    }
            // Where the first number is the absolute maximum
            // number of that thing that can be loaded in the
            // area, the keyword (mob|obj) to denote what type
            // of thing you are limiting, and the last number is
            // the number IN YOUR MOB or OBJ file padded with 0's
            // and with the leading XXX's.  (So mob 12 in your
            // file becomes XXX012, yes you need leading 0's,
            // XXX1 will not give you mob 1)

<commands>  // Commands are the word "in" followed by a list of rooms (or
        // just one room) and a list of commands (or just one command)
        // in the following format:

    in room_list subcommand
    in room_list { subcommands }

        // A room_list is a specified format of rooms to perform the
        // command(s) in.  Rooms are specified in the following
        // ways:

    room                // load in just this room
    (room)              // same as above
    (room1 | room2 | ... & roomn)   // load in one of room1, room2,...
                    // picked randomly (works like "OR")
    (room1 & room2 & ... & roomn)   // load in ALL of room1, room2,...
                    // (works like "AND")
    (room1 - roomn)         // all of room1 through roomn
    (room1 |- roomn)        // loads in ONE OF room1 through roomn
                    // same as specifying all of room1 |
                    // room2 | room3 ... up to roomn

        // A subcommand is the word "load" followed by a long set
        // of modifiers and submodifiers.  This is probably the most
        // confusing part builders have to deal with.  The load command
        // works in the following format:

    load <object>
    load <mobile>

        // <object> and <mobile> are defined as follows:

    <object>
      [modifier] obj <obj_list> [containing <object>]
      [modifier] obj <obj_list> [containing { <objects> }]
    <mobile>
      [modifier] mob <mob_list> [with <object>]
      [modifier] mob <mob_list> [with { <objects> }]

        // Anything inside [] may be omitted (although some SHOULD
        // be included)
        // Anything inside <> refers to a previous definition
        // (obj_list and mob_list follow the same format as the
        // room_list) and an object can contain an object which
        // can contain another, etc...
        // If used, the {} must be there, and the [] and <> are NOT
        // to be included in the load command.

        // Modifier is one of the following:
        //  atmost x
        //  upto x
        //  atmost x prob%
        //  upto x prob%
        //  prob%
        // atmost x means load enough mobs/objs to bring the total
        // in the room list to "x" mobs (replace x with a number)
        // upto x means load ONE mob/obj if there are less than "x"
        // in the room list (replace x with a number)
        // the prob% is a number followed by a % (e.g. 50%) that will
        // limit the actual loads to that percentage of the time. The
        // load condition (atmost or upto) must be met as well.
        // If there is no %, 100% is assumed on all loads.
        // A percentage by itself loads that thing that percent of
        // the time.

        // A mob that loads with objects is smart enough to wear
        // or wield them, if it can.

END // All zones (waaaay back up at the ZONE "name" command you started
    // the zone, remember?) must end with an END word.  You may, in the
    // same file, add as many ZONEs (start with ZONE "name") as are
    // relevant to your area.  Each ZONE must have the four required
    // areas (ZONE, definitions, commands, END) in that order.

Some tips on writing a good zone file:

Use the boot_only section for "one time" mobs that are important to plot
or high-ranking officials.  A good rule of thumb, is any mob with a proper
name (i.e. not a generic "guard" or "citizen" etc...) should load ONCE and
only once, to simulate realism.  Since some realism has to be sacrificed, it
will suffice that these boot_only things will load once in a blue moon when
the computer cold boots (enough time, that should someone kill a key mob
and have to wait for a reboot, they will think twice the next time they want
to get experience from a plot mob.)

Each ZONE in your file should use the range and save definitions across the
entire range (i.e. If it's in the range, it should be in the save as well.)
This will preserve your zone across warm boots.  Also choose a realistic value
for the reset definition.  Don't make a major city repop every 2 minutes, and
by the same token as the 'boot_only' tips, it would be logical to not have
it reset at all (reset never)  At the very least, put a long reset time on
the zone (over 60 minutes.)

Make your load commands compact, yet easy to read.  Simplify by putting
many redundant loads together:

    in 87 load atmost 1 mob XXX002 with obj (XXX045 & XXX046)
    in 89 load atmost 1 mob XXX002 with obj (XXX045 & XXX046)
    in 99 load atmost 1 mob XXX002 with obj (XXX045 & XXX046)

like this:

    in (87 & 89 & 99) load atmost 1 mob XXX002 with obj (XXX045 & XXX046)

Objects can be made to load inside objects, so you can have a mob load with
a pouch that contains a key, perhaps:

    zone_limit {
      2 obj XXX002  // pouch
      1 obj XXX003  // key
      1 mob XXX001  // the mob
    }
    in 204 load {
      atmost 1 10% mob XXX001 with 50% obj XXX002 containing obj XXX003
      20% obj XXX002
    }

This one gets a little explanation, as it shows off alot of the syntax.  First
the zone_limit defines the absolute maximum of the mobs/objs that can be
loaded inside the zone (not to be confused with max_exist for objects.)
Then, the command loads in room 204, all that stuff.  That stuff, which is
inside the brackets, is mob #1 in your mob file at a 10% chance.  He has a
50% chance (if he loads at all) to load with a pouch, which if it loads will
always contain the key.  There is also a 20% chance that the pouch will load
empty on the floor.

You can have mobs load with random objects, in random rooms, in different
ways each time.  Consider the following example:

    zone_limit {
      20 mob XXX002  // soldiers
      10 mob XXX003  // people
      2 mob XXX023   // something else
    }
    in (204 |- 210) load atmost 1 50% mob XXX023 with obj XXX002
    in (220 - 240) load atmost 1 mob XXX002 with {
      50% obj XXX004
      50% obj XXX005
      10% obj XXX006
    }
    in (210-220) load {
      upto 1 25% mob XXX003 with 50% obj (XXX010 | XXX011)
      atmost 1 mob XXX003
    }

A bit cryptic, but read through it a few times.  First, the limits are defined
for each type of mob.  Then, in one room out of the group 204 through 210,
there is a 50% chance that one mob #23 will load with object #2.  Next, in
all the rooms 220 through 240, one mob #2 will load in each one.  He will have
a 50% chance of loading with object #4, a 50% chance of loading with object #5
and a 10% chance of loading with object #6.  So 50% of the 20 will have object
#4, 50% will have #5, and 10% will have #6 (roughly.)  Note that the objects
will be spread out among all mobs in that load statement.  Next, this is the
complicated one.  If there is less than 1 mob #3 in each of the rooms 210-220,
one will load with a 50% chance of having either object 10 or 11 (that's a
50% chance of EITHER one, not of BOTH.)  Then, enough will be loaded to bring
the total of mob #3's up to 1 IN THAT ROOM.  So if one didn't load from the
previous statement, it will from this one.  You will get one mob #3 in each
room, but there is a 25% chance that they will load with something, and a 75%
chance that they will be barehanded.

Here are a few full sample ZONE files from PR1's zone files.  See if you
can figure them out from what you read above.


ZONE "Limbo"
  range (0 - 49)
  reset never

  boot_only {
    in 3005 load mob 1
    in 2    load obj 3097
    in 15   load obj 3097
  }
END

ZONE "Misc rooms"
  range (50 - 100)
  reset never
END

ZONE "The Ambush"
  range (400 - 414)
  reset (always, 11)

  in 1 {
    load atmost 5 mob 4112 with obj (51 | 50 | 47)
    load mob 4113 with obj 3091
  }
  in 3 load mob 4112 with obj 49
  in 6 {
    load atmost 4 mob 4112 with {
      obj (48 | 47)
      25% obj 42
    }
    load atmost 3 mob 4113 with obj 52
    load mob 261 with obj (3091 & 48)
  }
  in 13 load obj 413            // blade trap
  in 14
    load mob 4000 with  // snake
      obj (5032 & 5239 & 3056 & 3056)
END

ZONE "Asgard"
  range (1000 - 1099)
  reset never

  in 0 load obj 3098  // board
  in 1 load obj 3094  // player rulings board
END

(Note these do not have XXX's because the XXX's are there only for the
submission account to properly fix your room numbers into the entire PR2
zone files.)

The common gems that you find all around the realms follow the same pattern
in name and quality. These gems can be combined to create a more powerful
version of the gem. To reach the most powerful level you will need a lot.

You may also find unique and more powerful sockets when you kill powerful
foes throughout the realms. A socket with the flag (Unique) can only be
worn on one item. If another item contains the same Unique gem, they cannot
be equipped at the same time.

Each gem gives different attributes and some might be more beneficial for
certain classes or playstyles.

Here are the common gem types:
diamond  -> Increases your overall stats and life draining.
amethyst -> Increases your damage reduction and hitpoints.
emerald  -> Increases your magical abilities and spell damage.
topaz    -> Increases your speed and melee attack damage.
ruby     -> Increases your arts of stealth and assassination.

